{
  "image_name": "0a330ec915e122796676309f2e09d95c.jpg",
  "prompt_text": "The figure summarizes two ways telomere-associated proteins act outside telomeres to control gene expression. In the left model, RAP1 appears to link telomere biology with inflammatory and metabolic regulation. It interacts with the NF-κB pathway upstream of the p65/p50 complex and IκB, promoting activation of NF-κB target genes in the nucleus. The same scheme also shows RAP1 leaving the telomere-associated complex with TRF2 and binding elsewhere in the genome, where it influences genes such as Pgc1a and Ppara, suggesting broader control of metabolic programs.\n\nThe right model focuses on TRF2 and neuronal gene regulation. Here, REST is shown binding a neuronal silencer element and repressing neuronal genes, while TRF2 or a short form, TRF2-S, modulates this repression. TRF2 is also depicted with REST4 in the nucleus and is shown both at telomeres and at a separate genomic site labeled within the HS3ST4 locus, indicating direct extra-telomeric binding to neuronal and immunomodulatory genes.\n\nOverall, the image emphasizes that telomere proteins such as RAP1 and TRF2 are not confined to chromosome ends but also participate in signaling and transcriptional regulation across the genome.",
